From: Marcin Krystianc <marcin.krystianc@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] SUNRPC: account for Reply pages the encode stream consumed

svcauth_gss_wrap_integ() appends the integrity checksum to the Reply
with xdr_stream_encode_opaque(). When the Reply already fills the
current page, as an NFSv4.2 READ of rsize bytes does, that call
advances the stream's page_ptr onto the next page and writes the
checksum there.

Nothing then updates rq_next_page. As nfsd4_encode_operation() puts it,
"the xdr_stream primitives don't manage rq_next_page", so nfsd syncs it
after encoding each operation. The RPCSEC_GSS wrap runs later, from
svc_authorise(), and no auth flavour touches rq_next_page.

The checksum's page is therefore outside [rq_respages, rq_next_page),
so svc_rqst_release_pages() neither drops the reference nor NULLs the
slot, and svc_alloc_arg() refills only NULLed slots. The page is handed
to the next Reply built by that nfsd thread, while svc_tcp_sendmsg()
has passed it to the socket with MSG_SPLICE_PAGES and the socket still
holds a reference. The first Reply goes out carrying the second Reply's
checksum.

The client then fails to verify the MIC and returns -EIO from
gss_unwrap_resp_integ(). Most occurrences are hidden by RPC retries;
the rest reach userspace as EIO on a READ.

Sync rq_next_page in svc_send(), once the Reply is complete: it is the
one place every server Reply passes after the auth wrap, and the layer
that releases and refills the pages. svcxdr_init_encode() starts
page_ptr at rq_next_page - 1 for every request, so the update is a
no-op unless the encode stream really consumed a page past
rq_next_page. It only ever moves rq_next_page forward: NFSv3
procedures advance rq_next_page ahead of the stream to reserve pages,
and those must not be un-reserved.

Observed with xfstests -g quick over NFSv4.2 with sec=krb5i, where 16
of 641 tests failed on reads with EIO. Reduced to a loop of 1 MB
O_DIRECT reads of a 4 MB file: 2 to 3 of 20 reads fail, and the trace
shows 5 to 11 rpcgss_verify_mic GSS_S_BAD_SIG events per run. In each
case the data the MIC covers is byte-identical on client and server,
while the token the client verifies carries the SND_SEQ of a Reply the
same nfsd thread signed one to three MICs later. Dropping
MSG_SPLICE_PAGES from svc_tcp_sendmsg() also makes the failures go
away, confirming the Reply is modified after being handed to the
socket.

With this change the reproducer reports no failed reads and no bad
MICs, and -g quick with sec=krb5i goes from 16 failures to 2, with no
EIO anywhere in the run. The two that remain are unrelated to this
path: generic/761 deliberately rewrites an O_DIRECT buffer during
writeback, so the integrity checksum over the Request cannot match,
and nfs/001 fails to set a large NFSv4 ACL with EINVAL under both
krb5i and krb5p.

Fixes: <commit that introduced MSG_SPLICE_PAGES in svc_tcp_sendmsg>
Signed-off-by: Marcin Krystianc <marcin.krystianc@gmail.com>
---
An alternative was to sync rq_next_page in svcauth_gss_wrap_integ()
itself, right after encoding the checksum. That also makes the
failures disappear, but no auth flavour manages rq_next_page today,
and a send-time sync covers any future late encoder as well.

Tested with knfsd and the NFS client in one UML kernel over loopback,
an MIT KDC in the guest and an xfs-backed export.

diff --git a/net/sunrpc/svc_xprt.c b/net/sunrpc/svc_xprt.c
index 40040af58..24a64482c 100644
--- a/net/sunrpc/svc_xprt.c
+++ b/net/sunrpc/svc_xprt.c
@@ -1003,6 +1003,15 @@ void svc_send(struct svc_rqst *rqstp)
 	xb->len = xb->head[0].iov_len +
 		xb->page_len +
 		xb->tail[0].iov_len;
+
+	/* The xdr_stream encode primitives don't manage rq_next_page. If
+	 * the Reply spilled onto a page past it, as the RPCSEC_GSS
+	 * integrity checksum can, account for that page here, lest it be
+	 * handed to the next Reply while the transport still references
+	 * it for this one.
+	 */
+	if (rqstp->rq_next_page < rqstp->rq_res_stream.page_ptr + 1)
+		rqstp->rq_next_page = rqstp->rq_res_stream.page_ptr + 1;
 	trace_svc_xdr_sendto(rqstp->rq_xid, xb);
 	trace_svc_stats_latency(rqstp);
 
